      <span class="abstract-short has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.26535v1-abstract-short" style="display: inline;">
        The metric distortion framework asks voting rules to elect a candidate close to the voters using only their <span class="search-hit mathjax">rankings</span>. SimultaneousPluralityVeto elects from the plurality <span class="search-hit mathjax">veto</span> <span class="search-hit mathjax">core</span> and achieves the optimal worst-case distortion of 3, but it is not Pareto-efficient: it can declare&hellip;
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.26535v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'inline'; document.getElementById('2610.26535v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'none';">&#9661; More</a>
      </span>
      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.26535v1-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        The metric distortion framework asks voting rules to elect a candidate close to the voters using only their <span class="search-hit mathjax">rankings</span>. SimultaneousPluralityVeto elects from the plurality <span class="search-hit mathjax">veto</span> <span class="search-hit mathjax">core</span> and achieves the optimal worst-case distortion of 3, but it is not Pareto-efficient: it can declare a tie between two candidates even when every voter prefers one of them. Removing Pareto-dominated candidates afterward would break the convexity of tied winner sets. We instead introduce regularization, which adds a small $\varepsilon$ to every candidate&#39;s weight, restoring Pareto-efficiency. To study its effect on distortion, we introduce the <span class="search-hit mathjax">rank</span> <span class="search-hit mathjax">score</span> <span class="search-hit mathjax">veto</span> <span class="search-hit mathjax">core</span>, whose candidate weights come from a positional <span class="search-hit mathjax">scoring</span> vector $\mathbf{s}$, and prove that its distortion lies in $[1 + 2(||\mathbf{s}|| - s_m),\, 1 + 2||\mathbf{s}||]$; we conjecture that the lower bound is tight. A fixed $\varepsilon$ can increase distortion, but letting $\varepsilon$ depend on the number of voters preserves the distortion guarantee of the original <span class="search-hit mathjax">veto</span> <span class="search-hit mathjax">core</span>. Regularized SimultaneousPluralityVeto thus has optimal distortion, is Pareto-efficient, and has convex tied winner sets, resolving an open question of Kızılkaya and Kempe.
