        &hellip;across many systems and get answers in plain language; it is useful only if each answer can be trusted and explained. A production system of this kind attaches a confidence <span class="search-hit mathjax">score</span> to every answer it writes over a database and uses it to show the answer, show it with a warning, or withhold it. The&hellip;
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.17256v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'inline'; document.getElementById('2610.17256v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'none';">&#9661; More</a>
      </span>
      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.17256v1-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        Conversational analytics lets people ask questions of records spread across many systems and get answers in plain language; it is useful only if each answer can be trusted and explained. A production system of this kind attaches a confidence <span class="search-hit mathjax">score</span> to every answer it writes over a database and uses it to show the answer, show it with a warning, or withhold it. The <span class="search-hit mathjax">score&#39;s</span> weights, caps and thresholds were set by design. This paper audits that design. On the 1,534 questions of BIRD dev, answered by GPT-5.1 with a benchmark-adapted production prompt and five samples per question, the designed <span class="search-hit mathjax">score</span> separates right from wrong answers weakly (AUROC 0.624, 95% CI 0.595-0.652) and withholds 35.7% of correct answers. Nearly all of its <span class="search-hit mathjax">ranking</span> signal comes from agreement among the samples, and most of it is lost where agreement is combined by a minimum with a checklist that drops to zero on any unsupported claim, including claims in commentary. We froze a rule in which agreement with the shown answer is the <span class="search-hit mathjax">score</span> and deterministic checks act as <span class="search-hit mathjax">vetoes</span> and caps, and pre-registered a test on Spider dev before any Spider answer existed. The primary hypothesis held: AUROC rose from 0.566 to 0.725 (paired difference +0.159, 95% CI +0.097 to +0.221), and correct answers withheld fell from 49.0% to 8.8% with PASS accuracy 95.7%. Under one matching policy the choice of agreement statistic matters little; the gain comes from how agreement enters the <span class="search-hit mathjax">score</span>. Whether added checks help depends on how correctness is defined, while they always cost unqualified coverage. A pre-registered empty-result check did not meet its effect-size criterion on Spider. Correctness here means the <span class="search-hit mathjax">core</span> answer; labels are model-judged, and a blinded two-person audit, not yet complete, will be reported in a revised version.
